Freestanding stand-ins for the C++ standard headers and the folly headers
outside FlatCombining.h and SaturatingSemaphore.h that those two include:
only what they use, as an aarch64 Linux build with clang, libstdc++ and
glibc compiles it.

- <atomic> is built on clang's __atomic builtins, as libstdc++'s is.
- std::mutex is glibc's normal mutex: locking is an acquire
  compare-and-swap of 0 to 1, and if that fails, an acquire exchange of 2
  and a futex wait; unlocking is a release exchange with 0. A futex wait
  that would sleep records folly_test_mutex_blocked and ends the thread,
  as a wait on a folly futex does (below). Retrying instead made the
  combiner spin on the lock while the requester held it, and the search
  too big to finish.
- std::thread discards its function: the test runs the dedicated
  combiner's loop as a thread of its own.
- folly::IndexedMemPool is a fixed array of records, constructed when
  first allocated. The tests allocate their records in setup, before the
  threads start, so the pool's own synchronization plays no part.
- folly::Function stores the callable inline.
- The futex (folly/detail/Futex.h, folly/detail/MemoryIdler.h): a wake
  records folly_test_woken. A wait whose futex still holds the expected
  value would sleep: it records folly_test_blocked and ends the thread by
  branching to folly_test_exit, the address the test's thread function
  returns to. Otherwise it returns at once, as the futex system call does.
- Spinning (folly/synchronization/detail/Spin.h) tests the condition once.
- hardware_destructive_interference_size is 128, folly's value on aarch64
  when the standard library gives none.
